<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Madurai Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1335 CE – 1378 CE · capital Madurai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Muslim sultanate in Tamil country, founded by a rebel Delhi governor and visited by Ibn Battuta.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur's</a> campaigns, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> region, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mabar">Ma'bar</a>, through officials. In 1335 the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-ahsan-shah">Jalal al-Din Ahsan Shah</a> rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a> and declared independence, founding a sultanate based at Madurai.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultanate ruled a largely <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> population with a small Muslim military elite and was marked by instability and violence; several sultans were murdered or died after short reigns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalal-al-din-ahsan-shah">Ahsan Shah</a>'s daughter, visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-muhammad-damghani">Ghiyath al-Din Damghani</a>'s court around 1344 and described its cruelty and an epidemic that struck the army. Coins are a major source.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The rising <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> attacked from the north; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumara-kampana">Kumara Kampana</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukka-raya-i">Bukka I</a>, defeated the sultan around 1370, and the sultanate's last remnants disappeared by 1378. The campaign is celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhura-vijayam">Madhura Vijayam</a> by his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangadevi">Gangadevi</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/madurai-sultanate.jpg" alt="Madurai Sultanate" width="320" height="482"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Venu62 at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thanjavur_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1335 CE – 1378 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madurai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jalal al-Din Ahsan Shah, Ghiyath al-Din Muhammad Damghani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ma'bar Sultanate, Sultanate of Ma'bar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=madurai-sultanate" aria-label="Madurai Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6834 1807 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Madurai Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7154 2063L7084 1941M7154 2063L7154 2063M7154 2063L7018 1385M7154 2063L7088 1864"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7084 1941h0M7154 2063h0M7018 1385h0M7088 1864h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7154 2063h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7154 2063h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Madurai. <a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=madurai-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
